          "Ya'qub, peace be upon him, was a prophet from a family of prophets. His father was Ishaq, and his grandfather was Ibrahim, peace be upon them all. When death came to Ibrahim and Ya'qub, they left their sons the most important testament of all: to worship only One Allah and to remain submissive to Him. Ya'qub gathered his children and asked them whom they would worship after he was gone. The sons answered that they would worship the God of his fathers, Ibrahim, Ismail and Ishaq, the One God, and to Him they would submit. This is how the Qur'an tells us that the greatest inheritance of this family was not land and not wealth, but pure faith.",
          "Ya'qub had many sons. He loved his son Yusuf very much, because he saw in him light and purity. But Yusuf's brothers grew jealous of this love and decided to get rid of him. They took Yusuf away from home, and when they returned in the evening, they wept before their father and brought his shirt stained with false blood, saying that a wolf had eaten the boy. Ya'qub did not believe them. The Qur'an says that he answered calmly and with strength: their own souls had made this deed seem fair to them, and so for him there was only beautiful patience, and only from Allah could help be sought against what they were saying.",
